9 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485439 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485439 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485439 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186484890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485439 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a980bcf9-df31-5948-bb20-037aa185ddd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485460 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9365ada-91e3-54da-a0d9-612ba60324bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485465 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "df655b6c-59fb-5622-9ea9-612d1e33b110" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485467 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e753034b-662a-52aa-9360-b4d13194111c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485485 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(K|L)=0.25,$ $P(K)=0.75,$ and $P(L)=0.3,$ compute $P(L|K).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04948a98-4203-56b1-a1c8-9d3794661fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485485 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|K) & =\\frac{P(K|L)P(L)}{P(K)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.25)(0.3)}{0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.075}{0.75} \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $90%$ of those infected and $9%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $14%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected?  Round your answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10d543bf-463f-57f9-8fa9-461f96d806af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485497 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $90%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $9%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.9=0.1,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.09=0.91.\n$$\n\n- Since only $14%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.14\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.14=0.86.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)+P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.91)(0.86)}{(0.1)(0.14)+(0.91)(0.86)} \\\\  & ≈0.9824\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485498 #edb/ref 1071 "Two identical boxes contain marbles. The first box contains two black and four blue marbles. The second box contains one black and five blue marbles. A box is selected at random, and one marble is chosen from the selected box, also at random. If the selected marble is black, what is the probability that it came from the first box?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "111248ff-8060-5580-819b-222a636556c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485498 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is black. Then, we require $P(F|B),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|B)=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and four blue marbles, and the second box contains one black and five blue marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(B|{F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|F)P(F)+P(B|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485500 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke. $51%$ of the population are female. If a non-smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being female? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "181eb68d-e7ae-553c-958d-610b9662b27f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485500 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't smoke, and let $F$ be the event that they are female. Then, we require $P(F|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|N)=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|F)=1-0.17=0.83,\\,P(N|{F}^{′})=1-0.38=0.62.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.51,\\,P({F}^{′})=1-0.51=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|N) & =\\frac{P(N|F)P(F)}{P(N|F)P(F)+P(N|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.83)(0.51)}{(0.83)(0.51)+(0.62)(0.49)} \\\\  & ≈58%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485509 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485509 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485509 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485509 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular school, $65%$ of students attend a literature course, and $45%$ of students attend a mathematics course. If $50%$ of mathematics students also attend a literature course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen literature student attends a mathematics course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485509 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "242fce9f-3cc9-573a-8f6b-cabffe543cea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485509 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $L$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a literature course, and let $M$ be the event that they attend a mathematics course. Then, we require $P(M|L),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|L)=\\frac{P(L|M)P(M)}{P(L)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(L|M)=0.5,$ since $50%$ of mathematics students also attend a literature course.\n\n- $P(M)=0.45,$ since $45%$ of students attend a mathematics course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a literature course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|L) & =\\frac{P(L|M)P(M)}{P(L)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.45)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485533 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular school, $70%$ of students attend a history course, and $40%$ of students attend an art course. If $50%$ of art students also attend a history course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen history student also attends an art course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56a74bdd-02d9-5cb6-a536-9db069af271c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485533 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $H$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a history course, and let $A$ be the event that they attend an art course. Then, we require $P(A|H),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|H)=\\frac{P(H|A)P(A)}{P(H)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(H|A)=0.5,$ since $50%$ of art students attend a history course.\n\n- $P(A)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of students attend an art course.\n\n- $P(H)=0.7,$ since $70%$ of students attend a history course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|H) & =\\frac{P(H|A)P(A)}{P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.4)}{0.7} \\\\  & ≈0.29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485545 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $91%$ of those infected and $4%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $15%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is not infected? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7540b1df-730f-5d2d-b7a8-be03db2a980d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485545 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|T)=\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P(T|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $91%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.91,\\,P(T|{D}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $15%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.15,\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.15=0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|T) & =\\frac{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P(T|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.85)}{(0.04)(0.85)+(0.91)(0.15)} \\\\  & ≈20%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who doesn't eat after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8763b60a-8a9e-5a4f-9933-1a229bcc9ffb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485552 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $N$ be the event that a randomly selected person doesn't eat after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|N),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|N)=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N)}=\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(N|M)=0.05,\\,P(N|{M}^{′})=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|N) & =\\frac{P(N|M)P(M)}{P(N|M)P(M)+P(N|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.05)(0.49)}{(0.05)(0.49)+(0.32)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈13%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular allergy is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $98%$ of allergy sufferers and $4%$ of non-allergic patients tested positive for the allergy. Only $2%$ of the entire population has the allergy. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is an allergy sufferer? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93a07e59-f865-5d60-89bb-f9fb0b96134d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485558 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy  testing positive is $98%,$ and the probability of a  non-allergic person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.98,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $2%$ of the population suffers the allergy, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.02,\\,P({A}^{′})=1-0.02=0.98.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|T) & =\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.98)(0.02)}{(0.98)(0.02)+(0.04)(0.98)} \\\\  & ≈0.33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105980" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485560 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(D|E)=0.5,$ $P(D)=0.4,$ and $P(E)=0.1,$ compute $P(E|D).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9823650d-b646-5b44-9eff-2feb134e0f44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485560 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(E|D) & =\\frac{P(D|E)P(E)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.1)}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.05}{0.4} \\\\  & =0.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1071 "Two identical bins contain chips. The first bin contains four green and two yellow chips. The second bin has three green and one yellow chip. A bin is chosen at random, and a chip is selected from the chosen bin, also at random. If we know that the selected chip is green, what is the probability that it came from the second bin?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9db6fca8-e341-510d-8f9f-24e493c62cfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485562 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that the second bin is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected chip is green. Then, we require $P(S|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(S|G)=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${S}^{′}$ is the event that the first bin is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first bin contains four green and two yellow chips, and the second bin contains three green and one yellow chip, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|S)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P(G|{S}^{′})=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the bin is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=P({S}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S|G) & =\\frac{P(G|S)P(S)}{P(G|S)P(S)+P(G|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{17}{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(E|G)=0.15,$ $P(E)=0.75,$ and $P(G)=0.3,$ compute $P(G|E).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0c5c51a-9ae7-508b-bfcb-a9d35170be46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485564 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G|E) & =\\frac{P(E|G)P(G)}{P(E)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.3)}{0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.045}{0.75} \\\\  & =0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular school, $65%$ of students attend a history course, and $55%$ of students attend a language course.  If $45%$ of language students attend a history course, what is the probability that a randomly chosen history student also attends a language course? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2713c99-1478-532d-bcdd-d19b74d5beb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485569 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $H$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a history course, and let $L$ be the event that they attend a language course. Then, we require $P(L|H),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(L|H)=\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(H|L)=0.45$ since $45%$ of language students also attend a history course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.55$, since $55%$ of students attend a language course.\n\n- $P(H)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a history course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|H) & =\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.45)(0.55)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5791" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485570 #edb/ref 1071 "Two identical boxes contain marbles. The first box contains two black and three red marbles. The second box contains four black and three red marbles. A box is selected at random, and one marble is chosen from the selected box, also at random. If the selected marble is red, what is the probability that it is from the first box?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b40352ca-72af-5209-909f-bc2acbfcdd56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485570 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that the first box is selected, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected marble is red. Then, we require $P(F|R),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem. \n\n$$\nP(F|R)=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R)}=\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${F}^{′}$ is the event that the second box is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the first box contains two black and three red marbles, and the second box contains four black and three red marbles, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|F)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P(R|{F}^{′})=\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\n- Since the box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=P({F}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|R) & =\\frac{P(R|F)P(F)}{P(R|F)P(F)+P(R|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{3}{7})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1071 "In a particular city, about $60%$ of mornings are cloudy and $30%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $40%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edf69e26-0d8e-540c-b142-6a409309f520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485589 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4\\cdot 0.3}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
